  "context_pill": "Computation of Total Income for Lambda Ltd. A.Y. 2026-27",
  "example_summary": "Step-by-step computation ledger starting from net profit as per profit and loss to arrive at total income of Lambda Ltd. by adjusting PGBP additions, deductions, and Chapter VI-A deductions.",
  "source_provision": "### Statutory Summary Matrix: PGBP Adjustments & Provisions\n\n| Provision / Area | Statutory Section | Core Condition / Test | Statutory Treatment / Consequence |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Depreciation** | Section 32 | Assets used for business; Income-tax Rules vs Companies Act | Add back book depreciation; deduct Income-tax Rules depreciation |\n| **Provisions vs Actuals** | Section 36(1)(iv) / 36(1)(va) | Actuarial/unascertained vs actual payments within due date | Provisions disallowed; actual payments allowed |\n| **Wage & Periodical Revisions** | Section 37(1) read with ICDS X | Reliable estimate and reasonable certainty of obligation | Allowable if based on fair estimate; no adjustment if already debited |\n| **Capital Loss by Fire** | Section 28 / Section 37(1) | Destruction of capital asset by fire | Capital in nature; disallow and add back to business income |\n| **Political Contributions** | Section 37(1) / 37(2B) | Advertisement in souvenirs of political parties | Prohibited deduction for companies; add back to income |\n| **Statutory Dues Refund** | Section 41(1) | Cessation or remission of trading liability or refund of tax | Taxable as business income to the extent retained by assessee |\n| **Government Assistance** | Section 28(iv) / Subsidies | Grants, concessions or subsidies by Central/State Government | Taxable as business income unless specifically exempt |\n\n\n> [!IMPORTANT]\n> Provisions & Reserves: General provisions for gratuity or unascertained liabilities are inadmissible under PGBP unless actual payment conditions under Section 43B or specific statutory allowances are satisfied.\n\n\n> [!WARNING]\n> Statutory Dues: Any refund of tax or statutory liability previously allowed as deduction becomes taxable under Section 41(1) when refunded, unless refunded back to the actual customers.",
